<a id="p02a"></a>
### P02A — Set up SQLite storage and migrations

```text
Execute only P02A: Set up SQLite storage and migrations.

Read ProjectPlan's accepted SQLite decision, applicable repository instructions, and
the Shared execution rules in InitialPrompt. Inspect the mapped Beads issue and current
files; use saved evidence, not assumed chat history. Follow the shared handoff format
and stop after this task.

Prerequisites: P02. Verify its required evidence before dependent work.

Work:
1. Add a small storage module using Python's standard-library sqlite3, parameterized
   queries, and a server-owned SQLITE_DATABASE_PATH. Document separate local examples
   for service-requests.sqlite3 and pilot.sqlite3, and explicit absolute production paths.
   Reject public path selection, absent production databases, and unwritable storage;
   only an explicit initialization command may create a new database. Ignore database
   files, WAL/SHM sidecars, and backups in Git and keep them outside public assets.
2. Implement the ProjectPlan connection policy and verify its settings. Bootstrap WAL;
   apply foreign_keys=ON, busy_timeout=5000, and synchronous=FULL before transactions.
   Check the Python-linked SQLite build includes the documented WAL fix. Give each
   synchronous unit of work its own same-thread connection and close it deterministically.
   Keep blocking database work off async event loops without sharing a global connection.
3. Provide explicit transaction helpers, including BEGIN IMMEDIATE for read-modify-write
   units, guaranteed rollback/close on error, and bounded lock-contention handling.
   Do not hold transactions across network calls or retry external effects automatically.
   Add a numbered SQL migration runner with checksummed schema_migrations history and
   an explicit maintenance command. Initialize only database metadata here; application
   tables arrive in P05 and P12. Runtime startup checks schema compatibility.
4. Test the layer against temporary file-backed SQLite with the actual driver. Cover
   settings, independent connections, commit/rollback, foreign-key enforcement, lock
   timeout, restart, missing/read-only storage, and successful/failed/repeated migrations.
   Verify an already-applied migration with a changed checksum is rejected. Retain 100%
   coverage of the first-party layer; SQLite's own engine is a dependency, not our code.

Acceptance and verification: Document and run exact initialize, migrate, start, and
full-coverage commands on a synthetic file. Show the linked SQLite version and checked
PRAGMA settings. A failed transaction/migration leaves no partial committed change,
restarting preserves data, and invalid production paths fail visibly. Keep unit tests
isolated from real data and do not use only in-memory mocks to prove persistence.

Deliverable: Reusable SQLite connection/transaction and migration utilities, tested
configuration, a synthetic database initialization path, and clear Mac setup instructions.
```

